1
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:07,650
Used cooking oil goes down the drain, hardens in the pipes and blocks them.

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,800
Blocked pipes flood the same low-income streets, every season.

3
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:17,800
That oil is worth several times the cardboard catadores live on — and none of it reaches them.

4
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,800
A catador collects it at the door and scans the bottle's code:

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,800
how much, from where, when, who.

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:31,800
Pickups are pooled and verified at a neighbourhood hub — one contract-grade batch.

7
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:36,800
Sold under contract to several processors, never just one.

8
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:43,700
40% of the revenue goes to the catadores; the rest to households, local shops and a school course.

9
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:49,800
Every scan sharpens the next round: forecasts, routes, retention.

10
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:53,000
75,678 litres recovered a year.

11
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:56,800
The pilot pays for itself — R$1.27 of social value per R$1.

12
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:01,700
EcoCycle — a collection system for the last mile.
